{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` sits at **798 lines against the locked 800-LOC hard cap** — two lines of headroom. The next change that adds three lines to it fails CI, and there is no allowlist and no way to raise the limit (`.github/module-size-limit.txt` is locked; a contributor change to it fails the build by design).\n\nNot hypothetical: change 2359 (`bd66-28a4-fd31-4c9f`) hit it — 788 on main, +45, CI refused at 833 on all four platforms. It landed by relocating one function and compressing a comment, clearing the cap but leaving 798. Other in-flight changes are growing `src/rebar` modules concurrently.\n\n### What the size test enforces, and where RED begins\n\n`tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py::test_no_src_module_over_the_hard_cap` mirrors the CI gate and enforces **only the 800-line cap** — NOT the 200-500 target. So at 798 it is **already GREEN**: this work does not flip it RED to GREEN, it keeps it GREEN with room. Measured: 798 (today) PASSES; 801 (append three blank lines) FAILS with \"over the 800-LOC hard cap\"; 373 (after the split) PASSES. The RED boundary is exactly **three added lines** away. The proving evidence is the measured post-split LOC and the restored headroom, not a RED-to-GREEN flip.\n\n## The seam — it already exists in the file\n\n`gitutil.py`'s docstring already carves out a policy essay, \"Blocking policy under local git lock contention\". That is the boundary: **`gitutil` RUNS a git subprocess; the extracted layer DECIDES what happens when git's own locks are contended.** The dependency runs one way (`gitutil` -> `git_locking`).\n\n### Exactly what moves to `git_locking.py`\n\nFunctions: `_store_git_op_lock`, `_store_git_lock_path`, `fetch_coordination_lock`, `_resolve_common_git_dir`, `_acquire_fetch_coord_flock`, `_with_index_lock_retry`, `_reclaim_if_stale_index_lock`, `_augment_lock_exhaustion`, `_is_index_lock_error`, `_is_git_lock_error`, `_jitter`, `_backoff_sleep`, `_lock_backoff_s`, `_lock_retry_budget_s`, `_resolve_tracker_git_dir`.\n\nModule-level state the moved functions read as globals — it MUST travel with its readers or they see an absent global: `_INDEX_LOCK_STALE_S`, `_INDEX_LOCK_ATTEMPTS`, `_INDEX_LOCK_BACKOFF_S`, `_INDEX_LOCK_BACKOFF_CAP_S`, the `_STORE_GIT_LOCK_*` and `_FETCH_COORD_*` triples, `_LOCK_PATH_RE`, `_LOCK_REF_RE`, and the probes `_retry_probe` / `_reclaim_probe`.\n\n### What stays in `gitutil.py`\n\n`run_git`, `run_git_bounded`, `run_git_write`, `_with_transient_fault_retry` + its `_TRANSIENT_*` tables and predicates, `_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT`, `_AUTOMAINT_OFF`, `_ticket_dirs`, `_dir_is_archived`, `path_is_foreign_to_branch`, `discard_unmerged_paths`.\n\n### Re-exports `gitutil` must keep (production importers, enumerated by AST)\n\nEvery `from rebar._store.gitutil import <moved name>` in `src/`, found by parsing import nodes:\n\n| moved name | production importers |\n|---|---|\n| `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` | `_commands/bridge_repair.py:49`, `_commands/fsck.py:57`, `_commands/fsck_recover.py:20`, `_commands/fsck_repair.py:32`, `_commands/fsck_scan.py:43`, `_commands/tracker_maintenance.py:99` |\n| `_reclaim_if_stale_index_lock` | `_commands/fsck_scan.py:43` |\n| `_with_index_lock_retry` | `_store/event_commit_git.py:29` |\n| `_INDEX_LOCK_STALE_S` | `_store/event_append.py:103` |\n| `_backoff_sleep`, `_jitter` | `_store/lock.py:71` |\n| `fetch_coordination_lock` | `_snapshot/git_fetch.py:23`, `_store/sync.py:52` |\n\nAll covered by re-export; the check is that every `rebar` module still imports cleanly.\n\n### Test consumers, split by HOW they depend on a moved name\n\n**(a) `monkeypatch.setattr` on the module object — a mis-target RAISES** (`raising=True` is the default and the moved probes/tunables are deliberately NOT re-exported): `test_git_contention_9305.py`, `test_delete_init_index_lock.py`, `test_compact_index_lock.py`, `test_event_append_index_lock.py`, `test_fsck_index_lock_reclaim_toctou.py`, `test_stale_lock_reclaim_write.py`, `test_fetch_common_dir_coordination.py`.\n\n**(b) `monkeypatch.setattr` whose READER STAYED** — must NOT be retargeted: `test_git_outcome.py` (string form `\"rebar._store.gitutil._backoff_sleep\"`) and `test_gitutil_transient_write_retry.py` (`gitutil._backoff_sleep` / `gitutil.run_git`); their readers `_with_transient_fault_retry` / `run_git_bounded` stay put.\n\n**(c) `getattr` READ with a silent default — a SECOND vacuity channel that does NOT raise.** `test_delete_init_index_lock.py:24` and `test_compact_index_lock.py:22` both do `_STALE_S = getattr(gitutil, \"_INDEX_LOCK_STALE_S\", 300)`. Drop the re-export and these fall through to the literal `300` instead of failing. Worse, the real constant IS `300`, so comparing values proves nothing — the test looks identical either way. The proof needs a SENTINEL default: `getattr(gitutil, \"_INDEX_LOCK_STALE_S\", sentinel) is not sentinel`. `test_event_append_index_lock.py:40` and `test_txn_index_lock.py:21` read it through `event_append`, which re-exports from `gitutil`, so the same chain must hold.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` passes (it passes before the work too; no regression).\n- [ ] `gitutil.py` is within the 200-500 LOC target.\n- [ ] The new `git_locking.py` is within the 200-500 LOC target.\n- [ ] Every moved function body is byte-identical to its original, shown by `difflib` over the moved line ranges.\n- [ ] The retained half of `gitutil.py` differs only in its docstring and import block; no executable line changes.\n- [ ] The same test set passes before and after the split, with no test moving from passing to failing.\n- [ ] Every `monkeypatch.setattr` aimed at a symbol whose reader MOVED is retargeted to `git_locking`.\n- [ ] Every `monkeypatch.setattr` aimed at a symbol whose reader STAYED is left on `gitutil`.\n- [ ] Each retarget is shown to be load-bearing by the evidence described under Testing.\n- [ ] `gitutil`'s production importers require no change, shown by importing every `rebar` module without error.\n- [ ] Every `getattr(gitutil, <moved name>, <default>)` READ still resolves on `gitutil`, proven with a sentinel default rather than by comparing the value.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Compare moved line ranges against the new module with `difflib`; the diff must be empty apart from join whitespace.\n- **`monkeypatch.setattr` is the dangerous part and needs explicit proof.** A patch aimed at the wrong module is **silently vacuous**, not a loud failure: the test still passes while patching nothing. A green suite is NOT evidence. For each retargeted patch, demonstrate it still takes effect by ONE of:\n  - **Mis-target probe** — aim the patch at the module the reader left, and show the run fails loudly (`monkeypatch.setattr` defaults to `raising=True`, so a name absent from that module raises `AttributeError`).\n  - **Seeded mutant** — for a name that IS still present on both modules (`_backoff_sleep`), seed the behaviour the patch is meant to observe and show the correct target notices it while the old target passes vacuously.\n  - **Invocation probe** — for a patch deliberately LEFT on `gitutil`, record calls and assert the patched callable actually fired.\n- **`getattr` reads with a default are the second vacuity channel and need a DIFFERENT proof.** They do not raise on a missing name, they fall through — and when the default equals the real value (`300`), the fallback is invisible. Assert with a sentinel default that the name resolves on `gitutil`, and that it is the same object `git_locking` holds.\n- Grep for the module path and every moved symbol **as a STRING** (`monkeypatch.setattr`, `getattr`, `importlib`, docs references); an LSP cannot see these, so an empty semantic search is not evidence of absence. Enumerate the production importers by parsing import nodes, not by eye.\n- Run `make lint` (including the complexity ratchet: `new=0 increased=0`) and `make typecheck` to zero, plus `check_comment_hygiene`, `check_raw_git_writes`, `check_wall_clock_asserts`.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787974985941392001,"uuid":"ef246746-65d6-476c-937d-d9fbe222e9b1"}